<%= UiComponents::Lookbook::TableOfContents.call(page) %>

Site navigation whose items open rich panels of links - a marketing-style mega-menu.
[Appica docs](https://appica.dev/ui/components/react/navigation-menu)

<%= embed UI::NavigationMenuPreview, :default %>

## Usage

```ruby
NavigationMenu(aria_label: "Main") do
  NavigationMenuList do
    NavigationMenuItem do
      NavigationMenuTrigger { plain "Products"; NavigationMenuIcon() }
      NavigationMenuContent do
        ul(class: "grid w-96 grid-cols-2 gap-0.5") do
          li { NavigationMenuLink(href: analytics_path) { "Analytics" } }
          li { NavigationMenuLink(href: components_path) { "Components" } }
        end
      end
    end
    NavigationMenuItem { NavigationMenuLink(href: pricing_path) { "Pricing" } }
  end
end
```

- `NavigationMenu` - the `<nav>`; shares `variant:`, `size:`, `orientation:` and the trigger `icon:` with its parts.
- `NavigationMenuList` / `NavigationMenuItem` - the row of entries; an item holds a trigger + content, or one link.
- `NavigationMenuTrigger` - opens its item's panel on hover, click, Enter / Space or the arrow toward the panel.
- `NavigationMenuIcon` - the open / close indicator.
- `NavigationMenuContent` - the panel; lay links out inside however you like.
- `NavigationMenuLink` - a link, in a panel (a full-width row) or in the bar.

Panels are rendered on the server, hidden inside their items, so crawlers and no-JS visitors still get every link.
The `ui--navigation-menu` controller moves the open panel into a popup in the browser's top layer, places it under
its trigger (beside it when vertical, flipping for RTL and the viewport edge) and morphs the popup's size as you move
between items. For a menu of actions from a button use a DropdownMenu; for an app command bar a
[Menubar](<%= page_path(:menubar) %>).

